/*!
\htmlinclude plugin_jquery.zoom.html

\page tutorial_swift Identification of Swift parameters from tensile data  

[TOC]

Introduction  
============ 

This tutorial gives a step-by-step procedure for the identification of hardening parameters of a micro-scale \f$\tau(\gamma)\f$ hardening law,
such as Swift law and single- or double-stage Voce laws, from macro-scale experimental stress-strain data. Note that all hardening laws in VEF
are micro-scale laws and are thus defined on the slip-system level.
As example, we use macro-scale stress-strain data of a uniaxial tensile test along RD of a polycrystalline DX51D steel sheet sample to identify
the corresponding micro-scale Swift law.

\note
A similar procedure may also be followed for Voce law or any other micro-scale hardening law that prescribes \f$\tau(\gamma)\f$.
The methodology can also be readily adapted for identification against another (single) monotonic test.

The micro-scale Swift law postulates the Critical Resolved Shear Stress of a grain \f$\tau\f$ as a function of 
a single internal state variable, being the accumulated slip \f$ \gamma \f$ of that grain, as:
\f[
	\tau(\gamma) = \tau_0 \left(\frac{\gamma}{\gamma_0} + 1 \right)^n
\f]
This heuristic hardening law has 3 parameters: \f$\tau_0\f$, \f$\gamma_0\f$ and \f$n\f$.

In what follows 2 approaches to obtain \f$\tau(\gamma)\f$ from the macro-scale experimental stress-strain data \f$\sigma(\epsilon)\f$ are demonstrated:

<ul>
	<li> [Taking texture evolution into account] (@ref swift_texture_evol)\n
		Deformation-induced changes in texture that affect the stress-level of the macroscopic flow stress \f$\sigma(\epsilon)\f$ are considered.
	</li>
	<li> [Neglecting texture evolution] (@ref swift_texture_const)\n
		Texture is assumed constant during the macro-scale test.
	</li>
</ul>

The macro-scale stress-strain curve (also called identification test) is given in the file `examples\tutorial_swift\DX51D_uni0_exp.txt`. Its contents looks as follows:
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
		<img class="fullsize" src="DX51D_uni0_exp.png" alt="Experimental stress-strain data from 0° tensile test (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Experimental stress-strain data from 0° tensile test (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

\attention A set of micro-scale hardening parameters is identified
under assumption of specific multi-scale model configuration,
such as the [model type] (@ref model_type) (e.g. Alamel or Taylor) 
and the [definition of slip systems] (@ref slip_system_family).
Even if the hardening law is the same, the parameters are generally different
for different multi-scale model assumptions.
So in case several variants of multi-scale model are being studied,
it is important that the identification procedure is repeated
for every model variant.


Swift identification with texture evolution  {#swift_texture_evol}
===========================================

Basics of the identification strategy
-------------------------------------

The identification strategy consists of three steps:
<ol>
	<li> __Non-hardening VEF simulation of the identification test__\n
	
	A non-hardening VEF simulation is conducted first.
	It is hereby assumed that the Critical Resolved Shear Stress equals unity, independent of the degree of deformation.
	Quantities associated to this non-hardening reference material are tagged by superscript-star symbol (*).
	So for instance, we have the relation \f$ \tau^* \equiv 1 \f$, and the tensile stress and accumulated slip of the
	non-hardening material are written as \f$ \sigma^*_{xx} \f$ and \f$ \gamma^* \f$, respectively. The goal of the non-hardening simulation is to obtain the relationship \f$\sigma^*_{xx}(\gamma^*)\f$.
	</li>
	<li> __Swift parameter identification through curve fitting__\n
	
	Parameter identification of the \f$\tau(\gamma)\f$ hardening law is based on the *approximate* relationship between the experimental target tensile stress \f$\sigma_{xx}\f$ on one hand,
	and quantities of the non-hardening material on the other hand:
	\f[
	\sigma_{xx} \approx \sigma^*_{xx}(\gamma^*) \tau(\gamma^*)
	\f]
	In general, this relation is only approximate since the right-hand side neglects 
	local interaction effects between grain orientations (reflected by the factor \f$ \sigma^*_{xx}\f$)
	and strain hardening (given by the factor \f$\tau(\gamma^*)\f$).
	It has nevertheless proven to be a usefull relation in practice.
	In case of the of Swift law, expansion of the \f$\tau(\gamma)\f$ relation leads to
	\f[
	\sigma_{xx} \approx \sigma^*_{xx}(\gamma^*) \tau_0 \left(\frac{\gamma^*}{\gamma_0} + 1 \right)^n
	\f]
	Simultaneous identification of the three Swift parameters
	\f$\tau_0\f$, \f$\gamma_0\f$ and \f$n\f$ can be accomplished by, for instance, least-square fitting,
	i.e. the minimization of following function of the parameters:\anchor eq_leastsquarefit
	\f[
	f\left(\tau_0,\gamma_0,n\right)=\sum_{i} \left[ \sigma^{exp}_{xx,i} - \sigma^*_{xx,i} \tau_0 \left(\frac{\gamma^*_{i}}{\gamma_0} + 1 \right)^n \right]^2
	\f]
	Note that the experimental datapoints \f$\sigma^{exp}_{xx,i}\f$ and the non-hardening simulation output data 
	(\f$\sigma^*_{xx,i}=\sigma^*_{xx}(\gamma^*_{i})\f$ and \f$\gamma^*_{i}\f$) need to be known for the same set of deformation levels \f$i\f$. 
	</li>
	<li> __Verification of parameter identification__\n

	As the identification relationship is approximate, it is advised to check that a satisfactory simulation accuracy 
	is obtained for the identification test using the hardening parameters identified in previous step.
	</li>
</ol>

Let's now see how these steps work out for the example at hand.

Non-hardening VEF simulation of the identification test
-------------------------------------------------------
As a first step, we need to simulate the 0° uniaxial tensile test assuming a non-hardening material model.
For DX51D, a pre-made configuration file `sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.cfg` is available in directory `examples\tutorial_swift`.
It looks as follows:
\includelineno sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.cfg

We want to identify hardening parameters for ALAMEL multi-scale model (line 8)
with 24 BCC slip systems (line 9).
The non-hardening behavior is accomplished by the default hardening settings (line 11),
as can be cross-checked with @ref page_alamdmc_generic.

The 0° tensile test is easily configured with the \ref module_udsa.
We can see that, besides specifying a single, in-plane tensile test along 0° to RD (lines 16-19 and line 23),
we request that increments be made in equal intervals of 0.018 of tensile strain (lines 20 and 22).
This is far from a random choice; in fact it corresponds  exactly to the strain intervals
in which the experimental data (cf. file `examples\tutorial_swift\DX51D_uni0_exp.txt`) is available. 

Now let's run the simulation:
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC UDSA sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.cfg
</pre>

Swift parameter identification through curve fitting
----------------------------------------------------

The output file of the non-hardening VEF simulation is provided in `sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.uds` in directory `examples\tutorial_swift\results`.
Let's take a glance at the leading 5 lines:
\dontinclude sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.uds
\until 0.540350518E-01

We should now have all coefficients for [the least-square minimization problem] (@ref eq_leastsquarefit):
<ul> 
	<li> The experimental datapoints \f$\sigma^{exp}_{xx,i}\f$ for strain levels \f$i\f$ are located 
	in `examples\tutorial_swift\DX51D_uni0_exp.txt`.</li>
	<li> The same experimental strain levels \f$i\f$ are also listed in column 4 of `sid1687f_udsa_hard0_evol.uds`, 
	so we also have the corresponding \f$\sigma^*_{xx,i}\f$ datapoints in column 5. </li>
	<li> Wait a minute - how about the datapoints of homogenized accumulated slip \f$ \gamma^* \f$?
	On first sight, it does not appear to be listed in the `uds`-output file...
	But in fact we *do* know it, because for the non-hardening material with unity CRSS,
	\f$ \gamma^* \f$ is numerically identical to the accumulated work \f$ W^* \f$,
	which we can find back in column 7.
	It can be derived from the rate of work of a particular grain due to slip activity 
	on slip systems \f$s\f$, which is given by \f$ \dot{w} = \sum_{s}\tau_{s} \dot\gamma_{s} \f$. 
	For the non-hardening material with \f$ \tau^*_{s}\equiv 1 \f$, 
	it follows that rate of work in the grain equals the total slip rate of the grain. 
	The identity \f$ W^* = \gamma^* \f$ is then found after time integration and volume-averaging (i.e. homogenization).</li>
</ul>

Least-square fitting results in the following parameter set:
\f$\tau_0=43.38MPa\f$, \f$\gamma_0=1.35\space10^{-2}\f$ and \f$n=0.243\f$.

\note
This procedure requires only a single run of VEF, namely for the non-hardening simulation in order to obtain \f$\sigma^*_{xx}(\gamma^*)\f$.
The minimization can be afterwards performed with a least-squares solver of the user's choice.

Verification of parameter identification {#swift_texture_evol_ver}
----------------------------------------

The verification step needs only a slight modification to configuration file, 
see file `sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_evol.cfg` in directory `examples\tutorial_swift`:
\includelineno sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_evol.cfg
The [Swift law variant] (@ref hardening_model3) is defined in the lines 12-13.
Other modifications are a new output prefix (line 4), and the reduction of increment size (line 24) to generate denser points on the stress-strain curve.

The output file generated from running this simulation
is `sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_evol.uds`, also included in the directory `examples\tutorial_swift\results`.
As can be seen in figure below, the stress-strain behavior of the virtual material
compares very well to the experimental behavior:
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
		<img class="fullsize" src="DX51D_uni0_exp-fit_evol.png" alt="Identification result for Swift hardening parameter set with texture evolution (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Identification result for Swift hardening parameter set with texture evolution (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>


Swift identification with constant texture {#swift_texture_const}
==========================================

Basics of the identification strategy
-------------------------------------

The identification strategy consists of three steps:
<ol>
	<li> __Non-hardening VEF simulation of the identification test__\n
	Similar to the [identification procedure with texture evolution] (@ref swift_texture_evol) a non-hardening VEF simulation is conducted first.
	However, this time only for a single small increment to determine the initial yield stress. The goal of the non-hardening simulation is to
	obtain the yield stress for the same load direction as the macro-scale test assuming \f$\tau^*=1\f$ .
	</li>
	
	<li> __Conversion of macro- to micro-scale parameters__\n
	The yield stress obtained from the non-hardening simulation is then used to convert the macro-scale hardening parameters into the corresponding 
	micro-scale counterparts.
	Here it is assumed that the macro-scale hardening law is already known in the following form:
	\f[
	\sigma_{xx} = \sigma_{xx,0} \left(\frac{\epsilon_{xx}}{\epsilon_{xx,0}} + 1 \right)^n
	\f]
	
	In the present example \f$\sigma_{xx,0}=112.9\f$ MPa, \f$\epsilon_{xx,0}=0.0054\f$, \f$n=0.2461\f$.
	
	</li>
	
	<li> __Verification of parameter identification__\n
	Lastly, the micro-scale law is used to simulate the identification test and to verify the accuracy of the calibration.
	</li>
</ol>

Proceed as follows to carry out the necessary steps.

Non-hardening VEF simulation of the identification test
-------------------------------------------------------
Again we start with a 0° uniaxial tensile test simulation assuming a non-hardening slip systems. However, we apply only a very small plastic strain
of 0.0001, since we only need the initial yield point.
The respective pre-made configuration file is `sid1687f_udsa_hard0_const.cfg` :
\includelineno sid1687f_udsa_hard0_const.cfg

To run the simulation enter:
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC UDSA sid1687f_udsa_hard0_const.cfg
</pre>

Conversion of macro- to micro-scale parameters
----------------------------------------------
The output file of the non-hardening VEF simulation is `sid1687f_udsa_hard0_const.uds` and can be found in the folder `examples\tutorial_swift\results` :
\include sid1687f_udsa_hard0_const.uds

We are interested in the total uniaxial stress \f$\sigma_{xx}\f$ (column 5) of the first increment, which represents \f$\sigma^*_{xx,0}\f$. It allows converting the macro-scale
parameters into micro-scale parameters:
\f[
\tau_{0} = \frac{\sigma_{xx,0}}{\sigma^*_{xx,0}}
\f]
\f[
\gamma_{0} = \sigma^*_{xx,0}\cdot\epsilon_{xx,0}
\f]	
Note that the exponent \f$n\f$ remains the same. This leads to \f$\tau_{0}=43.40\f$ MPa, \f$\gamma_{0}=1.41\space10^{-2}\f$, \f$n=0.246\f$.

Verification of parameter identification
----------------------------------------

Using the micro-scale hardening law we can now simulate the uni-axial calibration test. The UDSA configuration used in this example can be found
in file `sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_const.cfg` and is identical to the case for [texture evolution] (@ref swift_texture_evol_ver) except for minor differences in the hardening parameters due to keeping the texture constant.
\includelineno sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_const.cfg

The simulation results (listed in file `sid1687f_udsa_SwiftS_const.uds`) reproduce the experimental data accurately:

<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
		<img class="fullsize" src="DX51D_uni0_exp-fit_const.png" alt="Identification result for Swift hardening parameter set with constant texture (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Identification result for Swift hardening parameter set with constant texture (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>


*/